  "response": "그 자리에서 말문이 막혔을 때의 당혹감이 아직 남아 있는 것 같네요. 준비를 해 갔는데도 말이 안 나왔다는 건, 그 순간이 꽤나 압박으로 느껴졌다는 뜻이기도 할 텐데요. 그 이후로 계속 부끄러움이 따라붙는 건, 그 장면이 아직 끝나지 않은 것처럼 느껴져서일 수도 있겠어요.\n\n교수님이 어떻게 보실까 걱정되는 마음이 가장 크게 느껴지나요, 아니면 스스로에게 \"왜 그때 말하지 못했을까\" 하는 자책 쪽이 더 무겁게 남아 있나요?",
  "reasoning": "The user is a counseling client who shared an experience about a research progress report meeting with their supervisor. They felt they couldn't adequately explain the comparison criteria for their experiment, and since then they've been feeling ashamed and worried about how their professor perceives them.\n\nLet me think about what would be most helpful here:\n\n1. They're expressing shame and worry about being judged\n2. They're self-blaming (\"준비를 더 했어야 했는데\")\n3. The core emotion seems to be shame + anxiety about evaluation\n\nI should:\n- Acknowledge the difficulty of that moment and the lingering discomfort\n- Not rush to reassure them (\"교수님 괜찮으실 거예요\")\n- Not immediately jump to problem-solving\n- Maybe gently explore the shame or the worry about the professor's perception\n- Keep it natural, warm, not formulaic\n\nI want to avoid:\n- Generic \"그랬군요, 많이 힘으셨겠어요\" type responses that feel mechanical\n- Jumping to \"다음엔 이렇게 하시면 돼요\"\n- Dismissing the worry\n\nI'll validate the experience of that moment (when words don't come despite preparation), acknowledge the lingering shame, and perhaps gently open a thread about what specifically feels most troubling — the shame itself or the worry about the professor's judgment. Or I could reflect on the self-blame (\"준비를 더 했어야 했는데\") and explore that gently.\n\nLet me write something that feels like a real counselor would say — warm, specific, not overly structured.\n",
